1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,510
Why has Warren Buffett held Coca-Cola for 38 years without selling?

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,301
The answer takes one calculator tap.

3
00:00:07,301 --> 00:00:10,840
We'll also work out what dividend you'd get if you bought today.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:14,241
Three seconds to pick a can from the fridge.

5
00:00:14,241 --> 00:00:15,009
In that time,

6
00:00:15,009 --> 00:00:18,970
Buffett's company collects about 80 dollars in Coca-Cola dividends.

7
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:23,932
27 dollars a second, about 2.3 million a day,

8
00:00:23,932 --> 00:00:25,690
848 million a year.

9
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:31,690
Yet all Buffett ever paid for this stake was 1.3 billion dollars,

10
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:33,450
between 1988 and 1994.

11
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:35,274
Not a cent more since.

12
00:00:35,274 --> 00:00:37,563
He hasn't paid for another can,

13
00:00:37,563 --> 00:00:40,000
and this much arrives every year.

14
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:42,850
We'll go through three parts.

15
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:47,050
One: what six-year-old Buffett saw in a 25-cent soda business.

16
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:51,480
Two: why he bought a stock that looked expensive by 1988's numbers.

17
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:54,540
Three: the real reason he still won't sell.

18
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:57,202
But here's the stranger part.

19
00:00:57,202 --> 00:01:00,990
The boy who first sold Coke took 52 years to buy the stock.

20
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:03,021
The story starts in 1936,

21
00:01:03,021 --> 00:01:05,510
in a small grocery store in Omaha.

22
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:08,450
Part one.

23
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:10,787
Omaha, Nebraska, 1936.

24
00:01:10,787 --> 00:01:13,231
At his grandfather's grocery store,

25
00:01:13,231 --> 00:01:16,444
six-year-old Warren Buffett started buying six

26
00:01:16,444 --> 00:01:18,470
bottles of Coke for 25 cents.

27
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:21,760
Then he sold them around the neighborhood for 5 cents each.

28
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:27,157
Sell all six and you have 30 cents: 5 cents of profit,

29
00:01:27,157 --> 00:01:29,200
20% on a 25-cent outlay.

30
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,842
Grandpa's store, neighborhood customers,

31
00:01:31,842 --> 00:01:33,063
25 cents of capital.

32
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:35,200
A very Buffett-like first business.

33
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,401
In his 1989 letter,

34
00:01:37,401 --> 00:01:40,720
Buffett looked back: even then he clearly saw

35
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:44,260
the product's extraordinary appeal to customers.

36
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:47,993
That's part one: a good business is one where

37
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:51,300
the same customer comes back to buy again.

38
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:54,919
Yet the boy who saw this then just watched

39
00:01:54,919 --> 00:01:57,060
Coca-Cola for the next 52 years.

40
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:58,286
In his own words,

41
00:01:58,286 --> 00:02:02,330
he watched Coke blanket the world and still overlooked the obvious.

42
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,910
Part two. Let's go to 1988.

43
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:10,413
Buffett wrote: only in the summer of 1988 did

44
00:02:10,413 --> 00:02:13,297
my brain finally establish contact with my eyes.

45
00:02:13,297 --> 00:02:15,520
He finally acted on what he had seen.

46
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,869
That year, at 57, Buffett began buying.

47
00:02:18,869 --> 00:02:22,921
By early the next year he'd spent just over 1 billion dollars,

48
00:02:22,921 --> 00:02:24,490
for 6.3% of the company.

49
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:27,641
It was the most Berkshire had ever put into a

50
00:02:27,641 --> 00:02:29,340
single company up to then.

51
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:34,132
By the numbers back then, it wasn't cheap.

52
00:02:34,132 --> 00:02:39,170
The stock traded at about 15 times a year's earnings.

53
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:41,168
For every 10,000 won of stock,

54
00:02:41,168 --> 00:02:43,145
about 670 won of yearly earnings,

55
00:02:43,145 --> 00:02:44,343
while long-term U.S.

56
00:02:44,343 --> 00:02:46,860
Treasuries paid about 900 won in interest.

57
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:48,727
He could have earned more just sitting in bonds,

58
00:02:48,727 --> 00:02:49,630
and still chose the stock.

59
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:52,877
And the stock had already risen an average of

60
00:02:52,877 --> 00:02:54,990
18% a year for the previous five years.

61
00:02:55,190 --> 00:02:57,391
Not a sale price. Full price,

62
00:02:57,391 --> 00:02:59,440
on the day the tag went up.

63
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,544
Did Buffett misread the calculator? No.

64
00:03:02,544 --> 00:03:05,970
He was looking at something other than this year's profit.

65
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:10,540
What he saw was how Coca-Cola makes money.

66
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:12,541
In most countries,

67
00:03:12,541 --> 00:03:16,010
Coca-Cola doesn't bottle or truck the drinks itself.

68
00:03:16,210 --> 00:03:19,508
It sells concentrate; bottling partners do the

69
00:03:19,508 --> 00:03:22,160
heavy work of filling and delivering.

70
00:03:22,970 --> 00:03:25,205
Others buy the plants and trucks; Coca-Cola

71
00:03:25,205 --> 00:03:27,440
collects for the brand and the concentrate.

72
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:30,776
So margins are thick: last quarter it kept 33

73
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:34,830
dollars of operating profit on every 100 dollars of sales.

74
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:38,885
Roberto Goizueta's team, in charge since 1981,

75
00:03:38,885 --> 00:03:42,070
was spreading this business across the world.

76
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:45,330
Buffett bet that these profits would grow year after year.

77
00:03:46,130 --> 00:03:47,326
Ten years later,

78
00:03:47,326 --> 00:03:50,465
Coca-Cola's net income was about 3.4 times

79
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:55,100
larger: from 1 billion dollars in 1988 to 3.5 billion in 1998.

80
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:56,290
That's part two.

81
00:03:56,290 --> 00:03:58,271
Expensive on that year's profit,

82
00:03:58,271 --> 00:04:01,367
but Buffett was pricing the profits still to come.

83
00:04:01,367 --> 00:04:03,100
For ten years, he was right.

84
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:06,623
So was it smooth sailing after that?

85
00:04:06,623 --> 00:04:07,530
Not exactly.

86
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:11,000
Let's go to 1998.

87
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,695
After its 1998 surge,

88
00:04:13,695 --> 00:04:19,108
Coca-Cola stock only got back near that peak 16 years later,

89
00:04:19,108 --> 00:04:19,830
in 2014.

90
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:22,770
Long enough for a newborn to reach high school.

91
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:24,756
In his 2004 letter,

92
00:04:24,756 --> 00:04:27,440
Buffett called it a big mistake not to sell

93
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,000
several large holdings during the bubble.

94
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,011
So why not sell?

95
00:04:32,011 --> 00:04:36,400
Because one thing kept moving through those 16 flat years.

96
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:40,201
The dividend: the cash a company hands its

97
00:04:40,201 --> 00:04:42,130
shareholders out of profit.

98
00:04:42,330 --> 00:04:46,760
Coca-Cola has raised it for 64 straight years since 1962.

99
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,636
This year it pays 53 cents a quarter,

100
00:04:49,636 --> 00:04:51,190
2.12 dollars a year.

101
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:55,217
Berkshire's Coca-Cola dividends grew from 75

102
00:04:55,217 --> 00:04:58,663
million dollars in 1994 to 704 million in 2022,

103
00:04:58,663 --> 00:05:01,010
and about 848 million this year.

104
00:05:01,210 --> 00:05:02,946
In his 2022 letter,

105
00:05:02,946 --> 00:05:07,150
Buffett said they rose as surely as birthdays.

106
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:08,001
Per day,

107
00:05:08,001 --> 00:05:13,540
that's from about 200,000 dollars in 1994 to about 2.3 million now.

108
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:17,560
Birthdays come once a year; this company sends a gift four times.

109
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:20,569
So what did 38 years of waiting earn?

110
00:05:20,569 --> 00:05:22,300
One multiplication tells you.

111
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,130
Part three.

112
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:30,513
By 1994 Berkshire held 100 million Coca-Cola shares.

113
00:05:30,513 --> 00:05:33,774
Two 2-for-1 splits, in 1996 and 2012,

114
00:05:33,774 --> 00:05:36,330
turned them into 400 million.

115
00:05:36,530 --> 00:05:39,135
1.3 billion dollars divided by 400 million:

116
00:05:39,135 --> 00:05:40,770
about 3.25 dollars a share.

117
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:45,224
Each of those shares pays 2.12 dollars this year.

118
00:05:45,224 --> 00:05:48,050
400 million times 2.12 is 848 million.

119
00:05:48,250 --> 00:05:50,328
About 65% of what he paid.

120
00:05:50,328 --> 00:05:53,844
A dividend that was about 6% of cost in 1994

121
00:05:53,844 --> 00:05:58,320
now returns almost the whole stake in a year and a half.

122
00:05:58,520 --> 00:05:59,283
Same soda,

123
00:05:59,283 --> 00:06:02,490
but only the 1988 buyer gets free refills.

124
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:05,677
But the 65% is partly an illusion.

125
00:06:05,677 --> 00:06:07,713
Sell for about $36.7 billion,

126
00:06:07,713 --> 00:06:10,100
put it into a stock yielding 2.3%,

127
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:13,540
and the yearly dividend comes out about the same.

128
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:15,557
So the real reasons are two.

129
00:06:15,557 --> 00:06:18,346
The gain is about $35.4 billion; 21% of it,

130
00:06:18,346 --> 00:06:19,579
about $7.4 billion,

131
00:06:19,579 --> 00:06:21,655
would go to corporate tax first,

132
00:06:21,655 --> 00:06:24,510
and what's left can't pay the same dividend.

133
00:06:24,710 --> 00:06:28,576
And a company customers return to like this is hard to find again.

134
00:06:28,576 --> 00:06:29,630
That's part three.

135
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:32,459
One uncomfortable question remains.

136
00:06:32,459 --> 00:06:34,720
Would buying today tell the same story?

137
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:35,596
Conveniently,

138
00:06:35,596 --> 00:06:38,350
the third-quarter report card came out on October 27.

139
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:41,830
Let's look at the October 27 release.

140
00:06:42,630 --> 00:06:47,238
Third-quarter revenue rose 7% to 13.4 billion dollars.

141
00:06:47,238 --> 00:06:51,590
Comparable earnings per share rose 11% to 91 cents.

142
00:06:51,790 --> 00:06:53,237
Unit case volume grew 5%,

143
00:06:53,237 --> 00:06:55,900
while price and mix rose just 1%: it sold more

144
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:57,347
without leaning on price.

145
00:06:57,347 --> 00:07:00,010
The company also raised its full-year outlook.

146
00:07:00,810 --> 00:07:04,974
Bottom line: buy today and there's no 65%.

147
00:07:04,974 --> 00:07:05,965
At $91.73,

148
00:07:05,965 --> 00:07:10,030
the yearly dividend is 2.3% of the price.

149
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:11,908
On 100 million won of stock,

150
00:07:11,908 --> 00:07:14,544
that's about about 231만 원 a year before tax,

151
00:07:14,544 --> 00:07:17,180
and U.S. dividends arrive with 15% withheld.

152
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:21,928
Here's the twist: today's numbers rhyme with 1988.

153
00:07:21,928 --> 00:07:25,887
The stock trades at about 30.2x a year's earnings,

154
00:07:25,887 --> 00:07:29,370
while the 10-year Treasury yields around 5%.

155
00:07:29,570 --> 00:07:32,382
On paper, bonds pay more per year again.

156
00:07:32,382 --> 00:07:36,880
We're standing at the same fridge Buffett stood at 38 years ago.

157
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:41,429
So the one question to ask: can profits grow as

158
00:07:41,429 --> 00:07:44,020
fast as they did after 1988?

159
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:47,673
Your yield on cost is this year's dividend

160
00:07:47,673 --> 00:07:49,710
divided by the price you paid.

161
00:07:49,910 --> 00:07:52,225
If the dividend grows 5% a year,

162
00:07:52,225 --> 00:07:55,770
in ten years it would be about 3.8% of your cost.

163
00:07:55,970 --> 00:07:57,073
On 100 million won,

164
00:07:57,073 --> 00:07:59,742
that's about about 376만 원 a year in ten years,

165
00:07:59,742 --> 00:08:00,380
before tax.

166
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:04,860
Buffett's 65% is that same math run for 38 years.

167
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:07,643
Of course this math could miss.

168
00:08:07,643 --> 00:08:11,930
Sugar taxes and the spread of obesity drugs could mean fewer sodas.

169
00:08:12,130 --> 00:08:14,041
A tax dispute with the IRS and weak

170
00:08:14,041 --> 00:08:16,770
emerging-market currencies could also cut profits.

171
00:08:17,570 --> 00:08:18,444
On ValueNote,

172
00:08:18,444 --> 00:08:21,738
which scores 600-plus companies daily by Buffett,

173
00:08:21,738 --> 00:08:23,755
Munger and Graham's standards,

174
00:08:23,755 --> 00:08:25,100
Coca-Cola scores 48.

175
00:08:25,310 --> 00:08:28,354
Its earning-power score is a high 86,

176
00:08:28,354 --> 00:08:30,410
but its price score is 5.

177
00:08:31,210 --> 00:08:34,030
Valued conservatively with no future growth,

178
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:36,913
it comes to $45.45; today's price is 2x that.

179
00:08:36,913 --> 00:08:40,630
A great company and a great price are different questions.

180
00:08:42,330 --> 00:08:43,230
Let's wrap up.

181
00:08:44,030 --> 00:08:46,127
1936: six bottles for 25 cents.

182
00:08:46,127 --> 00:08:48,766
1988: a purchase that looked expensive.

183
00:08:48,766 --> 00:08:51,810
1994: buying finished at 1.3 billion dollars.

184
00:08:52,010 --> 00:08:55,170
And 2026: 65% of the cost comes back every year.

185
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:57,804
Yet the real reasons he holds are taxes,

186
00:08:57,804 --> 00:09:00,240
and a company that's hard to replace.

187
00:09:01,030 --> 00:09:03,660
In his 1988 letter Buffett wrote: with

188
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:07,190
outstanding businesses and outstanding managements,

189
00:09:07,190 --> 00:09:09,890
our favorite holding period is forever.

190
00:09:10,690 --> 00:09:13,489
So the question to keep isn't buy or sell.

191
00:09:13,489 --> 00:09:17,154
Do customers keep coming back to the companies you own,

192
00:09:17,154 --> 00:09:19,620
and do their profits grow every year?

193
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:22,012
And by the way: while you watched this video,

194
00:09:22,012 --> 00:09:25,130
Berkshire collected about 14,000 dollars in Coca-Cola dividends.

195
00:09:25,930 --> 00:09:27,841
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

196
00:09:27,841 --> 00:09:28,841
please subscribe, like,

197
00:09:28,841 --> 00:09:29,970
and turn on notifications.

198
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:32,588
The story of the companies Buffett picked in

199
00:09:32,588 --> 00:09:34,120
Japan is worth watching too.

200
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:37,685
Which company you own keeps customers coming back?

201
00:09:37,685 --> 00:09:40,040
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
